.TH check "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
check \- check file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcheck [\-s] \fIfilesystem ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
check -- check file system
Usage:	check [ -s ] filesystem ...
Option:	-s	Salvage as much as possible, given the problems detected
.HE
.B check
checks the consistency of each named
.IR filesystem ,
by running
.B /bin/icheck
and then
.B /bin/dcheck
on it.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B check
only reports the problems it finds.
With
.BR \-s ,
.B check
also asks
.B icheck
and
.B dcheck
to repair what they can; if the filesystem ends up clean or fully
repaired (no unfixed errors remain),
.B check
clears that filesystem's dirty flag in its superblock, so it will
mount read/write again.
.PP
.B "mount \-c"
runs
.B "check \-s"
this way by itself, on a file system that was not cleanly unmounted,
just before mounting it.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/bin/icheck\fR	i-node consistency check
\fB/bin/dcheck\fR	directory consistency check
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR icheck ,
.BR dcheck ,
.BR mkfs ,
.B mount
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B check
exits \-2 (254) if an internal error occurred (for example,
.B icheck
or
.B dcheck
could not be run); \-1 (255) if unfixed errors remain; 0 if no
errors were found; and 1 if errors were found and fixed, in which
case the system should be rebooted.
.SH NOTES
With more than one
.IR filesystem ,
the exit status is the bitwise OR of the per-filesystem results.
